\details{
Locations and hospitals become \pkg{sf} POINT features in WGS 84
(EPSG:4326), and road routes come from \code{\link[osrm:osrmTable]{osrm::osrmTable()}}, which queries
the OSRM Table service for durations and distances. \code{dataset} and
\code{hospitals} can also be \pkg{sf} POINT objects in any coordinate reference
system: their geometry is transformed to WGS 84 with \code{\link[sf:st_transform]{sf::st_transform()}}.

Routing uses the \pkg{osrm} package's own server settings, the
\code{osrm.server} and \code{osrm.profile} options. By default these are the public
FOSSGIS server \verb{https://routing.openstreetmap.de/} and its \code{"car"} profile,
so no MooseR or private server is involved. That server allows at most one
request per second and no heavy use, so requests are spaced at least \code{delay}
seconds apart, also across calls; use it for light, interactive work.
Coordinates are sent to the server and may appear in its request logs. If
you set these options yourself before \pkg{osrm} is loaded, which would
normally overwrite them, your settings are kept. Routes are computed from
OpenStreetMap data, (c) OpenStreetMap contributors, under the Open Database
License; the default server is run by FOSSGIS e.V.

Ranking and output rules match \code{\link[=Moose_nearest_hospitals]{Moose_nearest_hospitals()}} with its default
OSRM engine. Hospitals are ranked by increasing route distance, then travel
time, then their row order in \code{hospitals}; every eligible hospital is
compared, with no straight-line prefilter. OSRM returns fastest routes, so
distances are not guaranteed to be the shortest possible road paths, and
times are estimates without live traffic. The two fixed hospitals always use
their coordinates in \link{alberta_hospitals} and never enter a custom
nearest-three candidate list. Repeated input coordinates are routed once,
and fixed-hospital routes reuse candidate routes with the same coordinates.
Unreachable candidates are excluded; if fewer than three are reachable, the
remaining ranks are \code{NA}. A failed request leaves all nine nearest-hospital
results of its locations \code{NA}, because an incomplete comparison cannot
establish the nearest hospitals; fixed-hospital results from successful
requests are kept. A failed or unreachable fixed destination has \code{NA} in its
two columns.

Differences from \code{\link[=Moose_nearest_hospitals]{Moose_nearest_hospitals()}}:
\itemize{
\item The default server differs: \code{\link[=Moose_nearest_hospitals]{Moose_nearest_hospitals()}} uses
\verb{https://router.project-osrm.org} with profile \code{"driving"}, so default
results can differ by more than rounding. \code{Moose_nearest_hospitals(server = "https://routing.openstreetmap.de/routed-car", profile = "driving")}
uses the same server as this function's default.
\item One request routes many locations at once: up to \code{max_table_size}
locations, and at most \code{max_table_size^2} location-hospital pairs. With
the 112 bundled hospitals and the default of 100, that is 89 locations per
request, instead of two requests per location. A failed request affects
every location in it.
\item \pkg{osrm} reports durations in minutes rounded to 0.1 minute, so travel
times are multiples of 6 seconds, and distances are rounded to whole
metres. \pkg{osrm} also sends coordinates with five decimal places (about
1 m). Results can therefore differ slightly from
\code{\link[=Moose_nearest_hospitals]{Moose_nearest_hospitals()}}, and hospitals whose route distances differ by
less than a metre can swap ranks.
\item \pkg{osrm} 6.0.0 and later report distances in kilometres and earlier
versions in metres; both are converted to metres.
\item Only OSRM through \pkg{osrm} is supported: there is no \code{engine},
\code{max_candidates}, \code{timeout}, \code{username} or \code{password} argument;
\code{osrm.server} and \code{osrm.profile} replace \code{server} and \code{profile}, and
\code{max_table_size} replaces \code{batch_size}. \pkg{osrm} has no timeout or
redirect settings, so the defaults of the \pkg{curl} package apply.
Messages printed by \pkg{osrm}, such as its note on units, are
suppressed. With \code{on_error = "stop"}, a failed request names its first
input row and how many other locations it held.
